<header><p class="st-kicker">protectorate · West Africa</p><h1 class="st-title">French Cameroon</h1><p class="st-meta">1916 CE – 1960 CE · capital Yaounde</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The larger, French-ruled part of former German Kamerun, governed under League and UN mandates, which became independent Cameroon in 1960 after a bitter insurgency.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>British and French troops conquered <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kamerun">Kamerun</a> in 1914-1916 and divided it, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> taking about four-fifths of the territory. In 1922 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/league-of-nations">League of Nations</a> confirmed French rule as a mandate, and after 1946 it was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a> trust territory. The capital was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yaounde">Yaounde</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>French administration used forced labour to build railways and roads and promoted cocoa, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coffee-economy">coffee</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timber">timber</a> exports. In August 1940 the territory rallied to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/free-france">Free France</a>. After 1948 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/union-of-the-peoples-of-cameroon">Union of the Peoples of Cameroon</a>, led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ruben-um-nyobe">Ruben Um Nyobe</a>, demanded independence and reunification with the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/british-cameroons">British Cameroons</a>. Banned in 1955, it waged an armed revolt that French forces suppressed brutally; Um Nyobe was killed in 1958.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> handed power to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahmadou-ahidjo">Ahmadou Ahidjo</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cameroon">Cameroon</a> became independent on 1 January 1960. In 1961 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/southern-cameroons">Southern Cameroons</a> voted to join it, forming a federal republic, while the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/union-of-the-peoples-of-cameroon">UPC</a> insurgency continued into the 1960s.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/french-cameroon.jpg" alt="French Cameroon" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: Original: Unknown Vector: SKopp · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_France.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1916 CE – 1960 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>protectorate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Yaounde</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Ahmadou Ahidjo</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Christianity, Islam, traditional religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>French, Duala, Ewondo, Fulfulde</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/cameroon">Cameroon</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Cameroun, French Cameroun, Cameroons under French Mandate</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1938&amp;focus=french-cameroon" aria-label="French Cameroon on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5000 2033 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where French Cameroon lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5320 2289L5303 2220M5320 2289L5256 2278M5320 2289L5054 728"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5303 2220h0M5256 2278h0M5054 728h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5320 2289h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5320 2289h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Yaounde. <a href="/atlas?year=1938&amp;focus=french-cameroon">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
